    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nThe Classic Land Rover Defender Still Refuses to Die: Defender Classic V8 Is Available in Australia\n16 photos\nPhoto: Land Rover\nThe fans of the iconic 1983 through 2016 Land Rover Defender from the Land Down Under have received a gift in November: the Defender Classic V8 is available for commission in Australia . That means only two things: exclusivity and high prices.\nLand Rover is doing a lot of things lately: electric versions of both the Range Rover and Range Rover Sport, a first-ever Range Rover GT, plus they signed an agreement with Stellantis to make their products in America, with a potential L663-based Defender pickup truck as the inaugural US-made vehicle. Also, the Defender nameplate might also evolve to include a ‘baby’ version, the rumored all-electric compact Defender.\nAnyway, these things aren’t interesting for a portion of the Land Rover fan base – as these enthusiasts only have eyes for the older products. No worries, as Land Rover has the Classic division and things like the older Range Rover models and the defunct Defender are still within reach for customers, if they have enough cash in the bank accounts for commission-based creations, that is.\nNow, for the first time, the Defender Classic V8 is available to commission in Australia, “offering clients the opportunity to create a Defender tailored to them.” For a price, of course, as adventurers, enthusiasts, and collectors need to shell out at least £209k, which is about $280k at the current exchange rate, excluding delivery and local taxes!\nAnyway, the program at least accommodates all wishes. So, customers can choose from any of the classic body styles: the 90 Station Wagon, Soft Top, and 110 Station Wagon, and more versions are set to follow. Customers will benefit from dedicated support from the specialists at Classic Works in Coventry, UK, and Essen, Germany, and Land Rover has created a distinctive Sunset Gold Soft Top as an immediate model for sale, aiming to illustrate “the breadth of personalization available through Defender Classic.”\nEach example will be created using donor vehicles originally produced between 2012 and 2016, “fully remastered from the ground up, re-engineered and upgraded,” and featuring a genuine Land Rover 5.0-liter gasoline V8 engine packing 400 hp. It’s paired with an eight-speed ZF automatic transmission, an in-house suspension setup, and Works Bespoke features like modern LED headlights, the Heritage Lighting Pack or Crystal Lighting Pack, and can even be equipped with features like the Explorer Pack.\nThe latter adds expedition-focused functionality, including a Ply Guys Camper Conversion, “turning the rear space of the cabin into a bespoke living quarter, while a roof-mounted tent box and ladder are also an option for those seeking off-road adventure.” There are also four-piston Alcon brakes, premium leather inside, a 4.8-inch touchscreen infotainment system with wireless Apple CarPlay and Android Auto, and more. Cool or not?\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nLand Rover Defender Classic V8 Land Rover Defender land rover Defender Classic SUV v8 pricing Australia\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Android Auto 18.1 Stable Version Now Available for Download As Google Says YouTube Is Coming",
